# Dependencies
node_modules/

# Build output
dist/

# Downloaded and generated assets.
# All of these are reproducible from a clean clone with:
#   npm run assets    (downloads ~90 MB from NASA/SSS/astronexus/three.js)
#   npm run catalog   (builds stars.bin and the 2K/4K texture pyramid)
# Keeping ~107 MB of binaries out of git keeps the history usable.
# Textures ARE committed. Solar System Scope's CDN filters by User-Agent and
# refuses datacenter IPs outright - a Render build got 403 HTML pages for all
# 21 files, wrote them to disk, and then crashed the TIFF converter. The set is
# 28 MB of CC BY 4.0 images that change roughly never, so committing them is
# far cheaper than making every deploy depend on a host that can refuse it.
# `npm run assets` still refreshes them locally.

# Character models are committed too (5 MB, MIT, from the three.js examples).
# Between these, public/tex and public/data the build is fully hermetic: it
# needs no network at all, so no upstream outage or bot filter can break a
# deploy. `npm run assets` still refreshes everything.

# The 34 MB HYG source CSV is NOT committed - stars.bin is derived from it and
# is only 3.3 MB, so the input is a local-only build artefact.
tools/raw/

# public/data is generated too, with one exception: the exoplanet archive is
# the slowest and least reliable dependency in the build (65 s at best, and it
# does drop connections). At 1 MB it is far cheaper to commit than to make
# every deploy wait on it. Delete the file and re-run `npm run assets` to
# refresh it.
public/data/*
!public/data/exoplanets.json

